# okhi build output. NOTHING under build/ is versioned.
#
# The remote used to carry the flashable images so a fresh clone could cut a
# release without compiling. That convenience is gone on purpose: those binaries
# are built from firmware/<variant>/esp/src/wifi_secret.h when it is present, and
# a development build therefore has a real SSID and password sitting in clear
# text inside okhi.bin and okhi.elf. Keeping them out of the index means it
# cannot happen by accident, ever, regardless of what any build script does.
#
# The trade is deliberate: to produce a release you now clone, build all five
# targets, and then run make_release.ps1. See "Releases" in CLAUDE.md.

# ESP-IDF generated config. Rebuilt from sdkconfig.defaults on every build and
# verified byte-identical, so versioning it only adds noise. sdkconfig.defaults,
# sdkconfig.ci* and partitions.csv stay in: those are the source of truth.
# sdkconfig.old is just idf.py's backup copy of the same generated file.
firmware/*/esp/sdkconfig
firmware/*/esp/sdkconfig.old

# Every build tree, in full.
firmware/*/esp/build/
firmware/*/rp/build/
firmware/uart_bridge/build/

# Claude Code working files. Kept local on purpose: CLAUDE.md is a set of notes to
# an assistant about this specific machine (toolchain paths, build quirks, what
# has already been tried), and .claude/ holds per-user settings. None of it is
# useful to anyone cloning the project, so it stays out of the public remote. The
# user keeps their own backups.
CLAUDE.md
.claude/

# Developer WiFi credentials. Never commit these. wifi_client.h is the tracked
# mechanism and stays empty; wifi_secret.h is the file that actually holds a
# SSID and a password, and it is deliberately a different name so that it has
# never been in the index and cannot be committed by accident.
firmware/*/esp/src/wifi_secret.h

# Release output. make_release.ps1 drops release/<yyyyMMddHHmmss>/ holding the
# built images plus a copy of stuff/last_esptool, which is hundreds of megabytes.
# None of it belongs in the history: it is all reproducible from the tree, and a
# release built for testing carries whatever was in wifi_secret.h at the time.
# Ignoring the whole directory means no release can be committed by accident,
# whatever it is named.
/release/

# Older releases used to land in a bare timestamp folder at the repo root. Kept
# so an existing working copy does not suddenly show them as untracked.
/20????????????/
